package wasmcloud:llm@0.1.0-draft;

/// The `wasmcloud:llm/imports` world gives a component access to language
/// models as a capability. Components targeting this world can:
///
/// 1. Generate chat completions, whole or streamed (`inference`).
/// 2. Embed text as vectors (`embeddings`).
/// 3. Discover which models their binding permits (`models`).
///
/// Every operation is an `async func`, so a component can keep many
/// generations in flight concurrently.
///
/// Which model answers is decided by the binding, not the component. A binding
/// is configured by the operator with these keys (backends may define more):
///
/// | key               | meaning                                                         |
/// |-------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------|
/// | `backend`         | which implementation serves the binding, e.g. `openai`,         |
/// |                   | `anthropic`, `openai-compatible`, or `local`                    |
/// | `endpoint`        | base URL of a remote backend                                    |
/// | `api-key`         | credential for a remote backend; supply it via `secretFrom`     |
/// | `model`           | default chat model                                              |
/// | `embedding-model` | default embedding model                                         |
/// | `allowed-models`  | comma-separated ceiling on what a request may name              |
///
/// `endpoint`, `api-key`, and `allowed-models` are the operator's: under
/// `workloadConfig: deny` a workload cannot set them, so it cannot point a
/// credential at a host of its choosing or lift its own spending limit.
///
/// A workload that needs more than one model imports `inference` under
/// `(implements <label>)` labels — say `cloud` and `local` — and the operator
/// declares a binding of the same name for each.
world imports {
  import types;
  import inference;
  import embeddings;
  import models;
}

/// What a plugin serving language models exports. A plugin that has no
/// embedding model, say, exports a narrower world of its own instead.
///
/// `types` is exported, not merely used: a host component plugin is matched to
/// a workload by what it exports, and the workload imports `types` alongside
/// the interfaces that use it, so the plugin has to answer for it too.
world backend {
  export types;
  export inference;
  export embeddings;
  export models;
}
